   $q$Both texts end by suggesting that flight changes how people see things. How do their endings differ?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The poem's crowd shrinks its daily troubles to a speck, while Clementine looks up at the sky for a quiet promise.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-texts"},{"text":"The poem's crowd decides flight is only a passing novelty, while Clementine resolves to become an aviator herself.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Both endings express regret that the fair was spoiled by the noise of the machine.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"The poem's crowd looks outward toward unknown future possibilities, while Clementine borrows the aviator's view to shrink her own worries.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The poem's crowd looks outward toward unknown future possibilities, while Clementine borrows the aviator's view to shrink her own worries.$q$, $q$The poem closes with the sky's "quiet promise," a hope pointed outward, while Clementine imagines her stand "as a white speck" and feels her troubles were "measured at the wrong size." The third choice swaps the two endings.$q$, 'draft', 'a97e75f1-088a-5fef-b542-64f75efb5484')
