-- Passage rg12-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('89168ac2-e95f-5718-b890-fee0b30602bf', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bookmobile Comes (1950s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Thursday Stop (a story, 1955)

Every Thursday, Ruthanne Osei drove the county bookmobile forty miles along gravel roads to the farms beyond Pell's Crossing. Her most faithful reader was Tobias Hartley, a lanky twelve-year-old who wanted only books about faraway places: deserts, harbors, mountain ranges whose names he practiced aloud while she stamped his card.

One week, after he returned a volume on the ports of the southern seas, Ruthanne handed him something different, a thin book on the rocks and rivers of their own valley.

Tobias frowned at it. "I already live here," he said. "There's nothing in this valley but mud and fence posts."

"Humor me," she said.

The next Thursday he was waiting at the crossroads before she arrived, holding a speckled stone in each hand. He wanted to know why one was striped and the other full of tiny shells, and whether it was true that an ocean had once covered the very field behind his house. He had walked the creek every evening, he said, and found a whole shelf of rock that the book described exactly.

Driving home that night, Ruthanne thought about the boxes of atlases and travel books she carried. She had always believed her job was to bring the world to children like Tobias. Now she suspected it was also to send them home again, seeing it for the first time.

Text 2: Crossroads, Thursday (a poem, 1955)

I hear it first: the grinding of the gears,
Then see the dust it raises on the hill.
The little van of shelves comes round the bend,
And all our quiet crossroads stands stock-still.

I carry back the deserts and the seas,
The cities lit like lanterns in the rain.
I trade them for a mountain and a ship,
And watch the van go rattling off again.

Our road runs only to the mill and back,
But I have crossed the ice, the sand, the foam.
The farthest place I've been is in my hands,
And every Thursday carries me from home.$q$, 'draft')
