   $q$The poem's line "The farthest place I've been is in my hands" and Tobias's two stones both involve holding something. How do these images differ in meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The poem's book proves the speaker has traveled widely in person, while Tobias's stones prove he has visited the ocean.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The poem's book carries the speaker away to distant places, while Tobias's stones show that his own field holds a distant past worth wondering about.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Both images show readers who are bored by books and would rather hold real objects.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The poem's book shows the speaker longing for the valley, while Tobias's stones show his wish to leave it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The poem's book carries the speaker away to distant places, while Tobias's stones show that his own field holds a distant past worth wondering about.$q$, $q$The speaker's "farthest place" is a book, a journey of the mind, while Tobias's shell-filled stone leads him to ask whether "an ocean had once covered" his own field. The speaker's road runs "only to the mill and back," so the trips are not literal.$q$, 'draft', '89168ac2-e95f-5718-b890-fee0b30602bf')
