   $q$Both texts treat the river's recovery as something that requires waiting. How does each present that waiting?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Text 1 shows the river waiting ninety years for the dam to fall, while Text 2 shows two volunteers counting for nine afternoons.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-texts"},{"text":"Both texts show the waiting as pointless, since nothing changes by the end of either text.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Text 1 shows the waiting as exciting from the start, while Text 2 shows it as bitter and hopeless.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"Text 1 shows human patience through nine empty afternoons, while Text 2 shows the river slowly clearing and herons waiting for what is coming.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Text 1 shows human patience through nine empty afternoons, while Text 2 shows the river slowly clearing and herons waiting for what is coming.$q$, $q$Yusuf and Mrs. Fairweather "counted nothing" for nine afternoons, and in the poem the river "ran clear" while "herons came to stand and wait." The ninety years belong to the poem, and the nine afternoons belong to the story.$q$, 'draft', 'f3773370-9e38-55ca-b9b8-ff228a411527')
