   $q$How do the two texts differ in where they find value in the night sky?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both texts find value only in a perfectly dark sky and dismiss whatever the city offers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstatement"},{"text":"Both texts find the night sky dull and prefer the lights of the city.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"Text 1 finds it in the little a city sky still shows, while Text 2 finds it in traveling far to see a full sky.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-texts"},{"text":"Text 1 finds it in traveling far to see a full sky, while Text 2 finds it in treasuring the little a city sky still shows.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Text 1 finds it in traveling far to see a full sky, while Text 2 finds it in treasuring the little a city sky still shows.$q$, $q$Nadia drives ninety miles to see the galaxy, while the poem's speaker says "One planet is not nothing" from a fire escape. The poem clearly values the city sky, so the overstatement choice fails.$q$, 'draft', '02105ba7-b9f2-5a4c-a9c7-a6649a325864')
