  ('b657c770-ba43-590c-a531-921f7afafbbb', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Nadia wonders whether neighborhood children "even knew it was missing," while the poem's speaker says "I know the rest is there." How do these lines differ in their treatment of the loss?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Nadia is certain the children know the sky is missing, while the speaker doubts that any stars exist beyond the glow.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-meaning"},{"text":"Both lines suggest the loss is unimportant, since few people ever look up at night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"Nadia worries about people who cannot miss what they never saw, while the speaker knows exactly what is missing and chooses to value what remains.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Nadia's line shows she has given up on the sky, while the speaker's line shows a plan to move to the country.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Nadia worries about people who cannot miss what they never saw, while the speaker knows exactly what is missing and chooses to value what remains.$q$, $q$Nadia's worry is that children may not know "it was missing," but the speaker has "read the charts" and still keeps an inventory, holding on "to every word." The speaker never doubts the stars exist.$q$, 'draft', '02105ba7-b9f2-5a4c-a9c7-a6649a325864')
